vidence":["trade publisher signal: Farrar, Straus and Giroux / Macmillan","major-award recognition"]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Rwanda","confidence":0.9}],"argument":{"present":false,"statement":"","confidence":0},"academicOrientation":{"score":35,"confidence":0.35},"profileConfidence":0.72,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"7673ebe36f2f9975b94ef7020748926af0f64ede77f233a455f04a1072736a56","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Politics & Government","secondarySubjects":["Race & Ethnicity","War & Military"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book centers on the political dynamics surrounding the genocide in Rwanda, exploring themes of ethnic conflict and the broader implications of state power, while also detailing the violent events themselves.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"d6fb93b1262badfcec78e240edeb2ab68dd4d21ec119ca014c4a710a93a5b6fb","summary":"An unforgettable firsthand account of a people's response to genocide and what it tells us about humanity. This remarkable debut book chronicles what has happened in Rwanda and neighboring states since 1994, when the Rwandan government called on everyone in the Hutu majority to murder everyone in the Tutsi minority. Though the killing was low-tech--largely by machete--it was carried out at shocking speed: some 800,000 people were exterminated in a hundred days. A Tutsi pastor, in a letter to his church president, a Hutu, used the chilling phrase that gives Philip Gourevitch his title. With keen dramatic intensity, Gourevitch frames the genesis and horror of Rwanda's \"genocidal logic\" in the anguish of its aftermath: the mass displacements, the temptations of revenge and the quest for justice, the impossibly crowded prisons and refugee camps. Through intimate portraits of Rwandans in all walks of life, he focuses on the psychological and political challenges of survival and on how the new leaders of postcolonial Africa went to war in the Congo when resurgent genocidal forces threatened to overrun central Africa. Can a country composed largely of perpetrators and victims create a cohesive national society?","displaySummary":"An unforgettable firsthand account of a people's response to genocide and what it tells us about humanity. This remarkable debut book chronicles what has happened in Rwanda and neighboring states since 1994, when the Rwandan government called on everyone in the Hutu majority to murder everyone in the Tutsi minority. Though the killing was low-tech--largely by machete--it was carried out at shocking speed: some 800,000 people were exterminated in a hundred days. A Tutsi pastor, in a letter to his church president, a Hutu, used the chilling phrase that gives Philip Gourevitch his title. With keen dramatic intensity, Gourevitch frames the genesis and horror of Rwanda's \"genocidal logic\" in the anguish of its aftermath: the mass displacements, the temptations of revenge and the quest for justice, the impossibly crowded prisons and refugee camps. Through intimate portraits of Rwandans in all walks of life, he focuses on the psychological and political challenges of survival and on how the new leaders of postcolonial Africa went to war in the Congo when resurgent genocidal forces threatened to overrun central Africa. Can a country composed largely of perpetrators and victims create a cohesive national society?","primaryTopic":"Genocide, Atrocity & Political Violence","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Politics & Government","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Politics & Government","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Politics & Government","mappedSubject":"Politics & Government","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book centers on the political dynamics surrounding th